---
// Lesson 5.3 — Into the Cave: Scene Loading & Mode Switch
// isPlatformer, scene-name switching, gravityScale, the genre change.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>5.3 — Into the Cave: Scene Loading &amp; Mode Switch</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You break the door, you walk in — and the game you are playing
    quietly becomes a different game. Today: how ONE player script
    runs as a top-down character on the surface and a platformer
    underground, decided by nothing but the scene's own name.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the branch in PlayerController.Start:</p>
    <pre><code>private bool isPlatformer;

private void Start()
{
    string sceneName = SceneManager.GetActiveScene().name;
    if (sceneName == "Platformer" || sceneName == "Start")
    {
        isPlatformer = true;
        rb.gravityScale = 0;
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Wait — read it again, slowly. "Platformer" and "Start" set
        isPlatformer = <strong>true</strong>... and then set gravity to
        <strong>0</strong>? That reads backwards — until you learn the
        starter's naming: the "Start" scene IS the top-down overworld
        (that is where you respawned in
        <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>), and "Platformer" is the cave
        scene. Top-down means NO gravity — a top-down character who
        falls is a broken top-down character. The bool's name is the
        confession: isPlatformer means "we are in a scene where the
        movement style is top-down-or-platformer, not pure physics."
        Trace which scene you are IN before you judge any branch.
    </p>

    <p>
        Predict: the player walks into the cave door area. What must
        happen to the WHOLE world to put you in a platformer — same
        scene with a camera pan, or a brand-new scene load? Where did
        you last see SceneManager.LoadScene, and who called it?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            One component behaving differently per scene is the
            <strong>instance</strong> idea: every scene gets its own
            Player object, each with the same script but different
            rules. What is saved between scenes (StatManager!) and
            what is born fresh — and why do coins survive the door
            but position cannot?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — open the cave.</strong> The starter ships a
    Platformer scene (Assets/Scenes — check it exists; if your fork
    names it differently, use YOUR name and update the string in
    Start). Wire the cave door's lever event
    (<a href="5.2.html">5.2</a>) or a new trigger to:</p>
    <pre><code>SceneManager.LoadScene("Platformer");</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it. The whole world is swapped in one frame. The Console
        from the overworld is gone, the enemies are gone, the coins
        are gone — everything except what a DontDestroyOnLoad
        singleton carries. Walk around: the same PlayerController
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — feel the mode.</strong> In the cave scene, try
    WASD: vertical input does something DIFFERENT now — or nothing,
    depending on how the branch is built. Read Update's
    isPlatformer branch: which input does the platformer branch
    ignore, and what does top-down use instead of AddForce for
    movement? Your movement table from <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a>
    gets a second column today.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the physics flip.</strong> Top-down:
    gravityScale 0, vertical ySpeed translation. Platformer
    physics: gravity ON, and jump via AddForce Impulse. Same script,
    opposite physics. Change gravityScale in the Inspector while in
    the cave — watch your character fall like a stone (2) or float
    like a moon astronaut (0.05). THAT is the number you will tune in
    <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a>.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The wrong-world player</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Rename the Platformer scene to "Cave" in the Project
                window but do NOT update the string in Start.
                Describe every symptom, then find it — how long did
                the silent-string hunt take this time?</li>
            <li>Load the Platformer scene directly (File → Play from
                that scene) without breaking the door. Does the game
                work — and why is that both a feature and a
                bug?</li>
            <li>In the cave, the E-key lever scan runs. Place a lever
                in the cave and press E. Is the interaction system
                overworld-only — should it be?</li>
            <li>The overworld keeps gravityScale 0 in Start. Delete
                that line and press Play in the overworld. Where did
                the player go — and why did "falls forever" look
                like "nothing happened"?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Mode design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Coins carry across the door (singleton) but your
                position does not. Design the respawn contract: if
                you die in the cave, where do you wake — and what
                does LoadScene("Start") from
                <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> already give you?</li>
            <li>Add a second cave scene ("Cave2") and a door deeper
                in. What strings must match now — and would a
                scene-name LIST be better than an if?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"5.3a: door opens, mode switches, physics flips"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the scene-name branch and the (confusing) isPlatformer/gravityScale pairing</li>
            <li>Load a scene from code and from an event</li>
            <li>Explain what survives a scene change (singletons) and what does not</li>
            <li>Tune gravityScale and describe what it changes about the genre</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: The Axe &amp; The Cave</a> — the scene-switch reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — SceneManager, gravityScale</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.1.html">6.1 — Jump: Gravity &amp; Impulse</a> — Unit 6 begins.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="5.3" title="5.3a Into the Cave: Scene Loading &amp; Mode Switch — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
